---
import brand from '@arcvia/brand'
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro'
---

<Legal
  title="Privacy policy"
  description={`How ${brand.name} handles your data and your clients' project files.`}
  updated="2026-08-18"
>
  <p>
    This policy covers {brand.name} — the marketing site, the studio editor, the
    render pipeline and any walkthrough you publish through us. It is written to
    be read, not to be survived.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>

  <h3>Account data</h3>
  <p>
    Your name, work email, organisation name and mobile number. The mobile
    number exists solely to send a one-time verification code; we do not use it
    for marketing.
  </p>

  <h3>Project data</h3>
  <p>
    Everything you upload or create: floor plans, CAD and PDF drawings, 3D
    models, textures, scene files and rendered output. <strong
      >This is your material and your clients' material, and we treat it as
      confidential.</strong
    > We do not use it to train models, we do not show it to other customers,
    and we do not publish anything unless you explicitly publish it.
  </p>

  <h3>Usage data</h3>
  <p>
    Which renders you queued, how much GPU time they used, and errors thrown by
    the application. This is what lets us bound compute cost and fix crashes.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do not collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Payment card details. When billing is enabled, card data goes directly to
      the payment provider and never reaches our servers.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your password. It is stored only as a salted hash and cannot be recovered
      by us — which is why a forgotten password is reset, not retrieved.
    </li>
    <li>
      Third-party analytics by default. Analytics is off unless an ID is
      configured for the deployment you are using.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where it lives</h2>
  <p>
    Account records and scene files are stored in object storage and a database
    in the region configured for your deployment. Rendering happens on GPU
    workers that receive a scene, produce an image, and retain nothing after the
    job completes.
  </p>

  <h2>Who else sees it</h2>
  <p>We share data with a small, fixed set of processors:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Processor</th><th>Purpose</th><th>What it receives</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Object storage / CDN</td>
        <td>Storing and serving scenes and renders</td>
        <td>Project files</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>SMS provider</td>
        <td>One-time verification codes</td>
        <td>Mobile number</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Email provider</td>
        <td>Password resets and service notices</td>
        <td>Email address</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Payment provider</td>
        <td>Subscriptions, once billing is enabled</td>
        <td>Billing name and address</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>We do not sell data, and we do not share it for advertising.</p>

  <h2>Published walkthroughs</h2>
  <p>
    A walkthrough you publish is served at a public URL. Anyone with the link
    can open it — that is the entire point of it. Treat a published link as
    public, and use the access controls if a project should not be.
  </p>

  <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Project files: until you delete them, or 30 days after you close your account.</li>
    <li>Render output: until you delete it.</li>
    <li>Usage and billing records: as long as required for tax and accounting.</li>
    <li>Password reset tokens: one hour, or until used.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask for a copy of your data, ask us to correct it, or ask us to
    delete it. Deletion of project files is immediate and irreversible — we do
    not keep a shadow copy. Write to <a href="/contact/">our team</a> and we will
    action it.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes materially we will say so in the product before the
    change takes effect, not quietly amend the date at the top.
  </p>
</Legal>
